<Page title="Editorial Policy: How We Write Emoji Meanings" description="Our editorial policy: how Allemojipedia researches, writes, reviews and corrects its emoji meaning pages and guides, including sourcing, use of AI tools and updates." path="/editorial-policy/" keywords={["editorial policy", "how emoji meanings are written"]} heading="Editorial policy" lead="How emoji meanings on this site are researched, written, reviewed and corrected.">
  <h2>What we publish</h2>
  <p>We publish two kinds of content: reference pages that explain a single emoji, and guides that cover a broader topic such as heart colors or emojis at work. We deliberately do not publish a page for every emoji. An emoji gets its own page only when there is enough to say about how people use it; the rest are available to copy from the <a href="/categories/">category pages</a>.</p>

  <h2>Facts versus interpretation</h2>
  <p>Each emoji page mixes two kinds of information, and we keep them distinct.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Facts</strong> such as the Unicode name, code points, the emoji version and year of release, category and shortcodes are taken directly from Unicode and CLDR data and are generated automatically, so they are not retyped by hand.</li>
    <li><strong>Interpretation</strong>, meaning what an emoji signals in a text, how tone shifts between age groups or apps, and when it can offend, is editorial judgment based on observed usage. Meanings vary between people and communities; we describe the common readings and say when one is niche, generational or regional.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Standards</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>We do not invent statistics, studies, quotes or rankings. If we cannot support a claim, we leave it out.</li>
    <li>Sexual, drug-related and other sensitive meanings are explained plainly and neutrally, because people look them up precisely to avoid misunderstandings.</li>
    <li>Flags and religious or political symbols are described factually. A flag emoji's presence on this site reflects its inclusion in Unicode, not a position on any territory's status.</li>
    <li>We describe platform designs only where differences are well documented.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Use of AI tools</h2>
  <p>We use AI writing tools to help draft and check content. Drafts are produced against a written brief that bans filler and unsupported claims, are validated automatically for structure and accuracy of references, and are reviewed by the editorial team. Responsibility for everything published rests with the team, not the tool.</p>

  <h2>Reviews and updates</h2>
  <p>Emoji usage moves quickly. Every page shows the date it was last reviewed. We revisit pages when a meaning shifts noticeably, when a new Unicode release changes something, or when a reader points out a problem. The date changes only when the content does.</p>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>If you believe something here is wrong or out of date, <a href="/contact/">tell us</a> and include the page address. We check every report and correct confirmed errors promptly.</p>

  <h2>Advertising</h2>
  <p>The site is funded by display advertising. Ads are served automatically by Google AdSense and are kept separate from editorial content. We do not accept payment for coverage, links or favorable descriptions.</p>
</Page>
